    "seoTitle": "Supporting a Young Athlete Without Adding Pressure | The Squad Sports Hub",
    "seoDescription": "Learn how to encourage effort, growth, and enjoyment without making your child feel that your approval depends on performance.",
    "isFeatured": false,
    "section": "parents",
    "content": "## Start With the Relationship\n\nChildren often experience pressure even when parents believe they are simply being supportive. Frequent score questions, sideline instructions, and visible disappointment can make sport feel like an evaluation instead of a place to learn.\n\nSupport is most effective when it protects the child’s ownership of the experience. Your child should know that you are interested in who they are becoming, not only in what they produce during competition.\n\n## What You Can Do This Week\n\n- Praise a specific behavior such as persistence, preparation, kindness, or courage.\n- Let the coach handle technical instruction during games and practices.\n- Ask your child what kind of support they want before, during, and after competition.\n- Keep family conversations broad so sport does not become the child’s entire identity.\n\n## Helpful Conversation Starters\n\n- **Try:** “What part of today felt most enjoyable?”\n- **Try:** “Was there a moment when you felt proud of how you responded?”\n- **Try:** “Would you like me to listen, encourage you, or help you think through something?”\n\nThese prompts are intentionally open-ended. Ask one, then leave enough quiet space for your child to answer without being corrected, coached, or rushed.\n\n## Signs That More Support May Be Needed\n\n- Your child repeatedly checks your reaction after mistakes.\n- Game days cause stomachaches, tears, or unusual irritability.\n- They describe sport mainly in terms of disappointing adults.\n\nOne difficult day is normal. A repeated pattern across several weeks deserves a calm conversation with your child and, when appropriate, their coach, school counsellor, physician, or another qualified professional.\n\n## The Parent's Role\n\nBe the person your child can look toward after both a great performance and a difficult one and see the same calm, welcoming expression.\n\nYou do not need to have every answer. Your most valuable contribution is being a steady adult who listens, protects perspective, and reminds your child that their worth never depends on a roster spot, statistic, or final score."
